f 17592186045417] [17592186482466 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482466 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186482303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482466 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a676cad1-550b-58ad-bc2d-4c3f63df1e8e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482531 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186482466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482531 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109579" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482531 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482531 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482531 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c788761-8a3d-5a84-9fc6-a55521c1f3bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482531 #edb/ref 1074 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-3I:$\n\n$$\nA-3I=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)v=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)w=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A-3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-3I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482630 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186482642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482630 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "378512a5-a4a3-5cfe-ac0c-8e6b41f621a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482630 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186482531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482633 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186482639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482633 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "50add218-dc98-586d-b53f-7221b90a299b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482633 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186413000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482639 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186482630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482639 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7ae106f8-dc27-52d1-9ba2-63f8ff076871" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482639 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186247001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482642 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "85151b46-52f9-5130-a26f-ee239d7c5fc8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482642 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186247012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186482647 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 